Change a network's subnet, router address or interface.

Changing the subnet renumbers the network: every client on it loses the
address it holds, and anything configured with a fixed address in the old
subnet stops being reachable. Use --dry-run to see the consequences first.

Usage:
  olr net set <name> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help                 help for set
      --ipv6-router string   this router's IPv6 address on the network (default: ::1 in the IPv6 subnet)
      --ipv6-subnet string   a static IPv6 /64 this router numbers the network from, e.g. 2001:db8:1:1::/64 (default: none — IPv6 comes from whatever prefix the interface already has)
      --member string        the interface the network lives on (default: the only adopted interface without one)
      --no-ipv6-subnet       drop the static IPv6 subnet, taking this router's IPv6 address off the interface
      --no-subnet            serve no IPv4 here, leaving the network to router advertisement alone
      --router string        this router's address on the network (default: the first address in the subnet)
      --subnet string        the network in CIDR form, e.g. 172.16.1.0/24 (default: the next free private /24)

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
